emainingAgentChanges` returns the `.claude/worktrees/...` files (in shadow tree, not committed) as `remainingFiles`.\n   - `carryForwardToNewShadowBranch` runs → **state reset to 0**.\n4. Turn 2.\n5. User commits `c8a97758` — same workflow file.\n6. PostCommit condenses 1bcdc44 reading `state.CheckpointTranscriptStart = 0` → metadata records 0.\n\nTwo corroborating fingerprints in the data:\n\n- The second checkpoint's session metadata has **no** `prompt_attributions` field at all. Carry-forward also zeros `state.PromptAttributions = nil` and `state.PendingPromptAttribution = nil` (`manual_commit_hooks.go:1380-1381`). Matches.\n- v2/main was populated by `entire migrate` (no `Finalize checkpoint:` commits paired with these two), so the stale 0 from v1 was faithfully copied through `computeCompactOffset` (`migrate.go:1061`), which short-circuits when v1's `m.GetTranscriptStart() == 0`.\n\n## So is #1120 the cause?\n\nNo — independent. #1120 is about the **finalize** step for **internal** agents not slicing the metadata to match the sliced transcript. Your case is the **condense** step for an **external** agent over-resetting state during carry-forward. Different function, different agent class, different direction of inconsistency.\n\n## The actual design bug\n\nThe \"self-contained checkpoint\" comment in `carryForwardToNewShadowBranch` is reasoning about internal agents whose transcripts are reconstructed from shadow-branch metadata. For external agents (Codex/Cursor/etc.), the transcript at condense time is **always** the cumulative agent session file. Resetting `CompactTranscriptStart = 0` makes every post-carry-forward Codex checkpoint claim authorship of the entire session-to-date, which:\n\n- breaks `checkpoint_transcript_start` as a \"where does this checkpoint begin\" index\n- silently double-counts work across checkpoints when consumers (explain, attribution, summaries) trust that index\n\nWorse: this fires whenever any uncommitted file remains after a Code